//! Use cases: orchestration, and nothing else.
//!
//! Each method does the same few things in the same order — check permission, call the
//! domain, persist, then publish what the domain raised. The business rules themselves
//! are one ring in, in `domain/`. If a rule starts being written *here*, it has leaked
//! out of the hexagon.
//!
//! These are plain methods rather than `jenny::UseCase` impls. A use case is a unit of
//! behaviour, and in Rust that is a function; the trait earns its keep only when you
//! need the indirection — several driving adapters that must not know the concrete
//! type, a heterogeneous set behind `Arc<dyn …>`, a decorator wrapping any of them.
